  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 2f78a0a3e840c3b488304b0ff928238c, type: 3}
  m_Name: BoxerLoreSO
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  LoreTitle: Boxer
  Lore: "I. \u201CWorth is determined by victory\u2026\u201D\n\r\n\tOne, two, punch.
    One, two, punch. Knockout! Boxer\u2019s life was defined by hard work and victory.
    Born to a city of metal, she dedicated her life to fighting. School was always
    a struggle and her creative endeavors never got far. Yet, she could take pride
    in being able to rip through anyone that stood in her way.\r\n\r\nWhen she first
    joined the arena, she was greatly underestimated. A girl of her stature and weight
    surely stood no chance against some of their greater champions. Yet, her punch
    was stronger than her appearance revealed. She became famous for her winning
    move\u2014a grand slam that sent anyone in her way flying across the ring.\r\n\r\nStarting
    from the first time she showed her face at the arena, a string of victories followed
    along. \r\n\r\n\r#\nII. \u201CThe bitter taste of defeat is a flavor I\u2019d
    rather forget\u2026\u201D\n\r\nEven the strong are susceptible to collapse in
    the face of something new. The world of metal that Boxer was born into was one
    of chaos and uncertainty. People made modifications to their bodies to make themselves
    stronger, smarter, more capable. A virus swept across the nation\u2014one that
    corrupted both the body and mind. Fearing that they might meet an untimely end
    regardless of how well they live their lives, body modifications became more
    common among the people of this world.\r\n\r\nA new challenger entered the arena
    and was one unlike anything the Boxer had ever seen in her life. His body was
    a grotesque arrangement of metal parts with bits of flesh patched in between
    them. Most people of the city were humans made of some metal. This challenger
    was metal made of some human.\r\n\r#\n\u201CHey, what\u2019s with this guy!\u201D
    Boxer protested, \u201CThought the rules were no body modifications.\u201D\r\n\r\n\u201CRules
    change.\u201D\r\n\r#\nIII. \u201CAppetite is the desire to be stronger\u2026\u201D\n\r\n\tFrom
    the moment that opponent stepped into the ring, a string of losses followed Boxer.
    The allowance of challengers with body modifications delivered a devastating
    blow to the competitors that adhered to the previous rules. What was once a valued
    skill to Boxer became a hindrance.\r\n\r\nUnable to keep up with these changes,
    Boxer contemplated the use of her own body. What\u2019s the point of fighting
    if I can\u2019t win? Even if my body is barely my own anymore, if I win, the
    win is still mine, right?\r\n\r\nThese thoughts consumed her until they became
    action.\r\n\r\nSickening, sickening, sickening. Ambition was not a word that
    could be used to describe the Boxer\u2014thirsty, hungry, insatiable. \r\n\r#\nIV.
    \u201CRust and decay are inevitabilities...\u201D\r\n\r\nHer body, now more metal
    than it was flesh, became a different monster. A string of victories followed
    her along with a trail of bodies. Giving up what she thought she valued made
    her realize what she really cared about\u2014a perfect win. \r\n\r\nWith her
    newly oiled joints and perfectly shined fists, she delivered blow after blow
    without feeling a single thing. \r\n\r\nOf course, such a thing cannot last forever.
    Over time, the Boxer began to feel the weight of her steel limbs and rust in
    place of natural decay.\r\n\r\nAnd one day, a new opponent\u2014younger, swifter,
    and smarter\u2014would put an end to her tyranny. One knockout and it\u2019s
    over.\r\n\r\n\r#\nV.  \u201CCan a life be won?\u201D\r\n\r\nWhen the Boxer arose
    from her comatose state, she was in a place that she recognized with fond familiarity.
    The arena was something she called home more than any other place or person\u2014it
    was her livelihood, her reason for living. \r\n\r\nWhen she realized where she
    had awoken, a wave of calm washed over her. The parts of her body where her flesh
    had been melted to fuse with metal no longer felt cold and foreign, the throbbing
    in her head had dissipated and the ringing she heard from time to time had gone
    silent. \r\n\r\nIf she was in the ring, nothing else mattered.\r\n\r\n\u201CBut
    you didn\u2019t win!\u201D a voice rang out. \r\n\r#\nIn the other corner of
    the ring was an opponent that she didn\u2019t recognize. Unlike the figures of
    metal that she had grown accustomed to, this opponent was weightless. A miasma
    of black smog taunted her, knowing that all she cared about was victory.\r\n\r\n\u201CGive
    me another chance. This time, I\u2019ll surely win.\u201D\r\n"
